
(All the tasks are run at once by saying make clean; make)

Task A can be run as
make clean
make 
./main A

AI was used for coding. 
A lot of code explanations are written in the code as comments. 

the Jacobi method only works for real symmetric matrices 
and to test the code I use one predefined symmetric 
matrix A and one random symmetric matrix B

What to look for is this 
V^T A V    should be diagonal 
V D V^T    should reproduce A
V^T V      should be the identity matrix
V V^T      should be the identity matrix
Some of the results are very small floating-point values
instead of 0 but they should be interpreted as numerical zero

task_A.txt contains the results of running ./main A


Task B is split into two parts. 
In the first part the following is done:
1. Reads rmax and dr
2. Builds the Hamiltonian 
3. Diagonalizes the Hamiltonian using Jacobi
4. Obtains the eigenvalues and corresponding eigenvectors
5. Saves the three lowest numerical eigenfunctions into 3 files wf0.txt, wf1.txt, and wf2.txt
6. Compares the three lowest energies with exact results and prints them.

It is run as
./main B -rmax 30 -dr 0.2   // the values 30 and 0.2 can be 
                            // chosen at will 


After the Jacobi diagonalization, the eigenvalues are 
sorted in increasing order and the eigenvector columns are 
reordered in the same way. This makes e[0] the lowest 
energy state, e[1] the first excited state, and so on.

this creates the files wf0.txt, wf1.txt and wf2.txt 
They are then plotted by running 
make plot_B

The eigenvectors returned by the matrix diagonalization 
are normalized as sum_i V_i^2 = 1. The physical radial 
wavefunction should satisfy the continuous normalization 
integral ∫ |f(r)|^2 dr = 1. On the grid this becomes
sum_i |f(r_i)|^2 dr = 1, so the numerical wavefunction is 
obtained as f(r_i) = V_i/sqrt(dr).

In the wavefunction plot, the numerical n=2 eigenfunction 
is multiplied by -1. This is not a correction of the 
physics, but only a sign convention. If v is an 
eigenvector, then -v is also an eigenvector with the same 
eigenvalue. The sign was chosen so that the numerical 
curve is easier to compare with the analytical one.

The next part of task B does the following:
1. Investigates the convergence with respect to dr by fixing rmax and testing different values of dr. The results are printed in the file dr_data.txt and plotted in the files plot_dr.svg and plot_drerror.svg
2. Investigates the convergence with respect to rmax by fixing dr and testing different values of rmax. The results are printed in the file rmax_data.txt and plotted in the files plot_rmax.svg and plot_rmaxerror.svg. 

The error plots use the exact hydrogen ground-state energy 
e0 = -0.5.
Therefore the plotted error is |e0 + 0.5|. As dr becomes 
smaller and rmax becomes larger, the numerical 
ground-state energy should approach -0.5.

It is run as
make plot_convergence

The chosen C task was this one:
"Check that the number of operations for matrix diagonalization scales as O(n^3) (by measuring the time it takes to diagonalize a random matrix of size n)."

It is run as 
make scaling
make plot_C

make scaling creates a file that contains two columns. 
The first column is N and the second column is the elapsed time. The Ns used are 40 60 80 100 120 140 160 180.
The data is created by running ./main C 40, ./main C 60 etc. This is done via the Makefile. 
The results are plotted in scaling.svg. The fit estimates the exponent b in time = a*N^b. For ideal O(N^3) scaling, b should be close to 3, which is also what we see. 

